<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<!-- The editor draws its own header instead of leaving it to the shell, like every other page in the
     app: the bar the shell draws looks different on every platform - and on Windows it renders no
     back button at all - while the way out of a page has to be in the same place everywhere. It is
     the header of the board this card came from, down to the padding and the spacing, so opening a
     card and closing it again does not move the arrow or the title. The title is the board the card
     came from, handed in by the constructor.
     The header carries the two ways out of the card as well - delete and done - which leaves the row
     of the tools under it to the controls alone. All three leave the card the same way and keep the
     ink either way, see OnDisappearing in the code-behind. -->
<ContentPage xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/2021/maui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2009/xaml"
             xmlns:markup="clr-namespace:Penban.Maui.Views.Markup"
             xmlns:ink="clr-namespace:Penban.Maui.Views.Ink"
             xmlns:controls="clr-namespace:Penban.Maui.Views.Controls"
             x:Class="Penban.Maui.Views.Pages.CardInkEditorPage"
             Shell.NavBarIsVisible="False"
             Style="{StaticResource AppPage}">

    <Grid RowDefinitions="Auto,Auto,*" Padding="12" RowSpacing="12">

        <!-- The same header as on the board the card was opened from: the way back, the name of the
             board it leads to, and the two ways out of the card. Delete and done stand up here rather
             than in the row of the tools, where they took the width the tools are drawn in: up here
             they cost the title a little room, and the title is the one thing that can give some up,
             because it truncates. They are the two buttons the board's picking row carries - the same
             glyphs, the same styles, in the same order - so leaving a card looks the same wherever it
             is done. -->
        <Grid Grid.Row="0" ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto,Auto" ColumnSpacing="6">
            <Button Grid.Column="0"
                    x:Name="BackButton"
                    Text="&#xf15c;"
                    Style="{StaticResource GhostIconButton}"
                    Clicked="OnBackClicked" />
            <Label Grid.Column="1"
                   x:Name="BoardTitleLabel"
                   Style="{StaticResource TitleLabel}"
                   LineBreakMode="TailTruncation"
                   VerticalOptions="Center" />
            <!-- Delete = U+F34D, done = U+F295. -->
            <Button Grid.Column="2"
                    x:Name="DeleteButton"
                    Text="&#xf34d;"
                    Style="{StaticResource DangerGhostIconButton}"
                    Clicked="OnDeleteClicked"
                    SemanticProperties.Description="{markup:Translate Delete}" />
            <Button Grid.Column="3"
                    x:Name="DoneButton"
                    Text="&#xf295;"
                    Style="{StaticResource AccentIconButton}"
                    Clicked="OnDoneClicked"
                    SemanticProperties.Description="{markup:Translate Done}" />
        </Grid>

        <!-- Everything that is a button or a colour lives in the one row above the note, so the note
             keeps the whole height underneath it: the drawing tools, the two pens and the paper
             colours scroll, and the way into the ring is pinned to the right end of the row, where it
             is always one press away. The two ways out of the card are up in the header instead, so
             this row belongs to the controls alone.
             There are two such rows, one upon the other and only ever one of them up: the tools of the
             pen, and the tools of the typed text. They stand in the same cell rather than one after the
             other, because exchanging them must not move what is pinned beside them - and each keeps
             its own scroll position, since a note is written in one mode for a while and switched back
             often enough that both rows want to be where they were left.
             The scrolling part is always drawn, whatever the tools setting says: the row is as high as
             the buttons pinned to its right end either way, so hiding the tools takes no height off the
             note, it only puts them out of reach.
             Drawing tools: one button for the two ways the pen draws, one for the eraser, and one each
             to undo and redo the last edit. Each button carries the glyph of the same tool that the
             radial menu draws on its ring, and the words that used to be on them are what a screen
             reader reads out. The font size is set here rather than left to the two styles so a button
             that changes between them does not change the size of its glyph with it.
             The pens come next, drawn as pens rather than as buttons, and they are what says which
             colour and which width the next stroke gets - see the slot styles in Theme.xaml. The two
             pens are built in the code-behind, one per stored slot, because the number of them and
             everything on them is a stored value rather than a decision this file can make. Behind
             them stands the button that wipes the note - one edit, so undo brings it back - and behind
             that the paper colours, each palette behind its own hairline. Horizontally scrollable,
             because the tools and the colours together are wider than any phone held upright. -->
        <Grid Grid.Row="1" ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" ColumnSpacing="12">
            <ScrollView x:Name="DrawingToolBar" Grid.Column="0" Orientation="Horizontal" HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Never">
                <HorizontalStackLayout Spacing="8">
                    <!-- Into the typed text and back out of it. It stands at the head of the row
                         because it exchanges the whole row for the other one, and it carries the glyph
                         of where it leads - the keyboard (U+F4B8) here, the pen (U+E8D8) in the row of
                         the text - so the two are one switch seen from either side. The words it is
                         read out with follow the same rule. Hidden for the board's own note, which is
                         written by hand alone. -->
                    <Button x:Name="TextModeButton"
                            Text="&#xf4b8;"
                            FontSize="20"
                            Style="{StaticResource GhostIconButton}"
                            Clicked="OnTextModeClicked"
                            SemanticProperties.Description="{markup:Translate TextModeOn}" />
                    <!-- The pen and the finger share one button: it carries the mode that is drawing
                         - a finger draws as well, or only the pencil does - and pressing it hands over
                         to the other one. The glyph of the two (pen U+E8D8, finger U+E6D8) and the
                         words it is read out with are put on in the code-behind, because that is what
                         tells the two modes apart. Eraser = U+E5E5, undo = U+F19A, redo = U+F16F,
                         clear = U+F201, radial menu = U+F07A5, pressure follows the pen = U+EF76,
                         lasso = U+F4C9, throwing the picked-up ink away = U+F34D. -->
                    <Button x:Name="DrawModeButton"
                            FontSize="20"
                            Style="{StaticResource GhostIconButton}"
                            Clicked="OnDrawModeClicked" />
                    <Button x:Name="EraserButton"
                            Text="&#xe5e5;"
                            FontSize="20"
                            Style="{StaticResource GhostIconButton}"
                            Clicked="OnEraserClicked"
                            SemanticProperties.Description="{markup:Translate Eraser}" />
                    <!-- The lasso: it draws a loop around ink while it is the tool in use, and what the
                         loop encloses is picked up to be carried elsewhere on the note. The button
                         beside it throws the picked-up ink away and stands only while there is any, so
                         the row says both what the tool is for and that it holds something. -->
                    <Button x:Name="LassoButton"
                            Text="&#xf4c9;"
                            FontSize="20"
                            Style="{StaticResource GhostIconButton}"
                            Clicked="OnLassoClicked"
                            SemanticProperties.Description="{markup:Translate Lasso}" />
                    <Button x:Name="SelectionDeleteButton"
                            Text="&#xf34d;"
                            FontSize="20"
                            IsVisible="False"
                            Style="{StaticResource GhostIconButton}"
                            Clicked="OnSelectionDeleteClicked"
                            SemanticProperties.Description="{markup:Translate DeleteSelection}" />
                    <Button x:Name="UndoButton"
                            Text="&#xf19a;"
                            FontSize="20"
                            Style="{StaticResource GhostIconButton}"
                            Clicked="OnUndoClicked"
                            SemanticProperties.Description="{markup:Translate Undo}" />
                    <!-- Undo and redo are also on two- and three-finger taps, so the buttons are the
                         visible half of the same feature. -->
                    <Button x:Name="RedoButton"
                            Text="&#xf16f;"
                            FontSize="20"
                            Style="{StaticResource GhostIconButton}"
                            Clicked="OnRedoClicked"
                            SemanticProperties.Description="{markup:Translate Redo}" />
                    <!-- A hairline, so the pens read as the other half of the same row rather than as
                         more tools. -->
                    <BoxView x:Name="PenSeparator"
                             WidthRequest="1"
                             HeightRequest="24"
                             VerticalOptions="Center"
                             Color="{AppThemeBinding Light={StaticResource BorderLight}, Dark={StaticResource BorderDark}}" />
                    <!-- The pens. Filled in from the stored slots in the code-behind; the one that is
                         drawing wears the frame that says so. -->
                    <HorizontalStackLayout x:Name="PenSlotRow" Spacing="2" VerticalOptions="Center" />
                    <!-- How wide the pen in hand writes, beside the pen it belongs to: the number is
                         the width it is set to, which neither the pen drawn in the row nor the ink on
                         the note says plainly, and the slider is what a width can be aimed at without
                         opening the flyout. The pen that is drawing is dragged for the same width as
                         well - see OnPenSlotPanned. The range is set in the code-behind, so the row and
                         the flyout take it from the one place a pen's range is written down. The row
                         leaves the hand-over of touches to the platform, so a flick along it scrolls it
                         wherever the finger lands and the slider is dragged by pressing it first:
                         taking that hand-over away would leave the row unscrollable under any of its
                         buttons. -->
                    <HorizontalStackLayout Spacing="2" VerticalOptions="Center">
                        <Slider x:Name="ToolbarThicknessSlider"
                                WidthRequest="96"
                                VerticalOptions="Center"
                                ValueChanged="OnToolbarThicknessChanged"
                                SemanticProperties.Description="{markup:Translate PenThicknessTitle}" />
                        <Label x:Name="ToolbarThicknessValue"
                               Style="{StaticResource CaptionLabel}"
                               WidthRequest="46"
                               HorizontalTextAlignment="End"
                               VerticalOptions="Center" />
                    </HorizontalStackLayout>
                    <!-- Whether the pen's own pressure is what decides the width. Lit while it does;
                         pressing it takes that away and every stroke is drawn at the width the pen was
                         given - the same switch the settings page carries, see OnPressureClicked. -->
                    <Button x:Name="PressureButton"
                            Text="&#xef76;"
                            FontSize="20"
                            Style="{StaticResource GhostIconButton}"
                            Clicked="OnPressureClicked" />
                    <!-- Wipes the note in one edit. It sits with the pens rather than with the paper
                         colours because that is what it acts on - the ink, not the note. -->
                    <Button x:Name="ClearButton"
                            Text="&#xf201;"
                            FontSize="20"
                            Style="{StaticResource GhostIconButton}"
                            Clicked="OnClearClicked"
                            SemanticProperties.Description="{markup:Translate ClearNote}" />
                    <!-- The paper colours sit behind a hairline of their own, so the two palettes are told
                         apart at a glance: the first one is the ink, this one is the note. -->
                    <BoxView x:Name="NoteColorSeparator"
                             WidthRequest="1"
                             HeightRequest="24"
                             VerticalOptions="Center"
                             Color="{AppThemeBinding Light={StaticResource BorderLight}, Dark={StaticResource BorderDark}}" />
                    <!-- Paper colours, filled in from the palette so a swatch is the note itself. -->
                    <HorizontalStackLayout x:Name="ColorPicker" Spacing="10" Padding="2" VerticalOptions="Center" />
                    <!-- Tags: emoji pinned to the note, filled in from CardTags.Palette in the
                         code-behind. Hidden for the board's own note, which cannot carry any. -->
                    <BoxView x:Name="TagSeparator"
                             WidthRequest="1"
                             HeightRequest="24"
                             VerticalOptions="Center"
                             Color="{AppThemeBinding Light={StaticResource BorderLight}, Dark={StaticResource BorderDark}}" />
                    <HorizontalStackLayout x:Name="TagPicker" Spacing="4" VerticalOptions="Center" />
                </HorizontalStackLayout>
            </ScrollView>

            <!-- The row that stands in the note's typed text: the way back to the pen, how large the
                 writing is and in what colour, and its two faces. Same shape as the row of the pen -
                 one scrolling row, the buttons that leave the card pinned beside it - so the two can
                 be exchanged without anything moving.
                 What is set here belongs to the card, not to the screen: the size in the same document
                 units the ink is measured in, the colour as a code on the card, and the two faces as
                 switches for the whole note rather than as marks on single words. The swatches are
                 built in the code-behind out of the very palette the pen uses, so a colour that is
                 picked is a colour wherever it is picked from, and the range of the slider comes from
                 CardText in the same way the pen's widths come from PenThickness. -->
            <ScrollView x:Name="TextToolBar"
                        Grid.Column="0"
                        IsVisible="False"
                        Orientation="Horizontal"
                        HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Never">
                <HorizontalStackLayout Spacing="8">
                    <!-- Back to the pen. The same switch as the button at the head of the other row,
                         seen from this side: this one carries the pen, that one the keyboard, and each
                         is read out with what pressing it leads to. -->
                    <Button Text="&#xe8d8;"
                            FontSize="20"
                            Style="{StaticResource GhostIconButton}"
                            Clicked="OnTextModeClicked"
                            SemanticProperties.Description="{markup:Translate TextModeOff}" />
                    <BoxView WidthRequest="1"
                             HeightRequest="24"
                             VerticalOptions="Center"
                             Color="{AppThemeBinding Light={StaticResource BorderLight}, Dark={StaticResource BorderDark}}" />
                    <!-- How large the writing is. The number beside it is the size the note is written
                         at, in points, which is what a size is usually read as; what is stored is the
                         size in the note's own units, so a note shows its writing in the same
                         proportion however large it is drawn. The range is set in the code-behind. -->
                    <HorizontalStackLayout Spacing="2" VerticalOptions="Center">
                        <Label Text="{markup:Translate TextSizeTitle}"
                               Style="{StaticResource CaptionLabel}"
                               VerticalOptions="Center" />
                        <Slider x:Name="TextSizeSlider"
                                WidthRequest="96"
                                VerticalOptions="Center"
                                ValueChanged="OnTextSizeChanged"
                                SemanticProperties.Description="{markup:Translate TextSizeTitle}" />
                        <Label x:Name="TextSizeValue"
                               Style="{StaticResource CaptionLabel}"
                               WidthRequest="46"
                               HorizontalTextAlignment="End"
                               VerticalOptions="Center" />
                    </HorizontalStackLayout>
                    <BoxView WidthRequest="1"
                             HeightRequest="24"
                             VerticalOptions="Center"
                             Color="{AppThemeBinding Light={StaticResource BorderLight}, Dark={StaticResource BorderDark}}" />
                    <Label Text="{markup:Translate TextColorTitle}"
                           Style="{StaticResource CaptionLabel}"
                           VerticalOptions="Center" />
                    <!-- The colours of the pen's palette, filled in from it in the code-behind: the
                         writing is written in the same colours the ink is drawn in. -->
                    <HorizontalStackLayout x:Name="TextColorPicker" Spacing="6" Padding="2" VerticalOptions="Center" />
                    <BoxView WidthRequest="1"
                             HeightRequest="24"
                             VerticalOptions="Center"
                             Color="{AppThemeBinding Light={StaticResource BorderLight}, Dark={StaticResource BorderDark}}" />
                    <!-- Bold (U+F7A4) and italic (U+F7F4) for the whole note; lit while they are on.
                         The title keeps its own weight either way, because that is what tells it apart
                         from the body - what the bold switch decides is the body. -->
                    <Button x:Name="TextBoldButton"
                            Text="&#xf7a4;"
                            FontSize="20"
                            Style="{StaticResource GhostIconButton}"
                            Clicked="OnTextBoldClicked"
                            SemanticProperties.Description="{markup:Translate TextBoldTitle}" />
                    <Button x:Name="TextItalicButton"
                            Text="&#xf7f4;"
                            FontSize="20"
                            Style="{StaticResource GhostIconButton}"
                            Clicked="OnTextItalicClicked"
                            SemanticProperties.Description="{markup:Translate TextItalicTitle}" />
                </HorizontalStackLayout>
            </ScrollView>

            <!-- The way into the ring and the settings. They stay outside the scrolling row so they
                 are always one press away: the ring is what a free finger taps on the note, and the
                 button is what is left when there is no free finger or no right mouse button. Without
                 the ring - the toolbar mode - the button that opens it is gone with it.
                 The settings are not a way out of the card, and the drawing settings are what is
                 tried out on this very note. It is the same button as on the overview and on the
                 board. The two ways out of the card are up in the header, see above. -->
            <HorizontalStackLayout Grid.Column="1" Spacing="8" VerticalOptions="Center">
                <Button Text="&#xf588;"
                        Style="{StaticResource GhostIconButton}"
                        Clicked="OnSettingsClicked"
                        SemanticProperties.Description="{markup:Translate Settings}" />
                <Button x:Name="MenuButton"
                        Text="&#xf07a5;"
                        FontSize="20"
                        Style="{StaticResource GhostIconButton}"
                        Clicked="OnMenuClicked"
                        SemanticProperties.Description="{markup:Translate RadialMenu}" />
            </HorizontalStackLayout>
        </Grid>

        <!-- The writing surface is the very note that sits on the board, in its own paper colour
             (set from the view model in the constructor). Strokes are stored dark-on-light.
             NoteArea is only a measuring cell: it reports the free space so the note can be kept
             square without the note ever feeding its own size back into the layout. -->
        <Grid Grid.Row="2" x:Name="NoteArea">
            <Border x:Name="NoteFrame"
                    Padding="10"
                    HorizontalOptions="Center"
                    VerticalOptions="Center"
                    Style="{StaticResource CardBorder}">
                <controls:StickyNoteBorder x:Name="NoteSurface" Padding="0">
                    <Grid>
                        <ink:InkCanvasHostView x:Name="InkHost" BackgroundColor="Transparent" />

                        <!-- The typed text of the card, laid over the note rather than beside it: the
                             note is the same square whether it carries strokes or letters, and the text
                             is measured in the same document units as the ink, so the two stay in
                             proportion however large the note is shown.
                             In the pen's mode the text stays in sight - so the user can write around
                             what is already there - but read-only, and every touch goes on to the ink
                             underneath. In the text field's mode it is the other way round, and then the
                             whole note is the field: a touch that misses the two of them puts the
                             cursor in the body text.
                             The fields are empty and left empty by this file; what is written in them
                             is the card's, read and written in the code-behind. -->
                        <ScrollView x:Name="TextSurface"
                                    IsVisible="False"
                                    Padding="14,10"
                                    VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Never">
                            <ScrollView.GestureRecognizers>
                                <TapGestureRecognizer Tapped="OnTextSurfaceTapped" />
                            </ScrollView.GestureRecognizers>
                            <VerticalStackLayout Spacing="2">
                                <Entry x:Name="TextTitleEntry"
                                       BackgroundColor="Transparent"
                                       Placeholder="{markup:Translate TextTitlePlaceholder}"
                                       TextChanged="OnTextChanged"
                                       Completed="OnTextTitleCompleted" />
                                <Editor x:Name="TextBodyEditor"
                                        AutoSize="TextChanges"
                                        BackgroundColor="Transparent"
                                        TextChanged="OnTextChanged" />
                            </VerticalStackLayout>
                        </ScrollView>
                    </Grid>
                </controls:StickyNoteBorder>
            </Border>

            <!-- The layer that takes a tap outside the flyout. It lies over the note but under the
                 flyout, and it is up exactly while the flyout is: a tap on the note puts the flyout
                 away instead of drawing, which is what makes the flyout behave like the popup it is
                 rather than one that has to be dismissed by pressing its own pen a second time. -->
            <Grid x:Name="PenFlyoutBackdrop"
                  IsVisible="False"
                  BackgroundColor="Transparent">
                <Grid.GestureRecognizers>
                    <TapGestureRecognizer Tapped="OnPenFlyoutBackdropTapped" />
                </Grid.GestureRecognizers>
            </Grid>

            <!-- The pen flyout: what the pen that is drawing is made of, opened by pressing that pen
                 a second time - the first press takes the other one up. It hangs downward from the top
                 of this cell - which is where the row above ends - so it covers the top of the note
                 rather than pushing it down, and it is placed sideways in the code-behind, under the
                 pen that opened it. It is the last child of the cell, so it lies over the note; the
                 ring is a child of the page and stays above both.
                 Every control in here writes into that same pen: there is one pen and one set of
                 controls for it, not a setting beside a pen, so the slot in the row and this flyout
                 can never disagree. The order runs from the least to the most effort - the colours
                 that were just used, the palette, a code, and then the two things a code cannot say:
                 which hue and how wide. The square and the bar are drawn here rather than bought in,
                 because a picker is a rectangle of colours and a mark on it. -->
            <Border x:Name="PenFlyout"
                    IsVisible="False"
                    HorizontalOptions="Start"
                    VerticalOptions="Start"
                    Padding="14,12"
                    WidthRequest="272"
                    Style="{StaticResource CardBorder}">
                <ScrollView VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Never">
                    <VerticalStackLayout Spacing="10">
                        <!-- Which pen this is. Set in the code-behind, because it is the pen's number
                             and that is not known here. -->
                        <Label x:Name="PenFlyoutTitle" Style="{StaticResource BodyLabel}" />

                        <Label Text="{markup:Translate PenRecentColors}" Style="{StaticResource CaptionLabel}" />
                        <HorizontalStackLayout x:Name="RecentColors" Spacing="8" />

                        <Label Text="{markup:Translate PenPalette}" Style="{StaticResource CaptionLabel}" />
                        <!-- Filled in from the same palette the ring draws, so a colour is in the same
                             place whichever of the two was used. -->
                        <FlexLayout x:Name="PenPalette" Wrap="Wrap" Direction="Row" />

                        <!-- A colour from anywhere: what the palette cannot hold. The swatch beside the
                             field is the colour the code says, so it can be checked before it is used.
                             A code that cannot be read is not applied - the pen keeps what it had - and
                             the field says so underneath instead. -->
                        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" ColumnSpacing="10" RowDefinitions="Auto,Auto">
                            <Border Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0" Padding="10,0" Style="{StaticResource InputSurface}">
                                <Entry x:Name="PenHexEntry"
                                       Placeholder="{markup:Translate PenHexPlaceholder}"
                                       TextChanged="OnPenHexChanged" />
                            </Border>
                            <Border x:Name="PenHexSwatch"
                                    Grid.Row="0"
                                    Grid.Column="1"
                                    WidthRequest="34"
                                    HeightRequest="34"
                                    StrokeShape="RoundRectangle 8"
                                    StrokeThickness="1"
                                    Stroke="{AppThemeBinding Light={StaticResource BorderLight}, Dark={StaticResource BorderDark}}" />
                            <Label x:Name="PenHexError"
                                   Grid.Row="1"
                                   Grid.ColumnSpan="2"
                                   IsVisible="False"
                                   Text="{markup:Translate PenHexInvalid}"
                                   Style="{StaticResource PenHexError}" />
                        </Grid>

                        <!-- The hue, then how strong and how bright it is. Both mark where the pen's
                             colour is now, and both write back into the pen while they are dragged. -->
                        <controls:HueStripView x:Name="HueStrip" HeightRequest="26" />
                        <controls:ColorAreaView x:Name="ColorArea" HeightRequest="112" />

                        <Label Text="{markup:Translate PenThicknessTitle}" Style="{StaticResource CaptionLabel}" />
                        <!-- The width, continuously: there are no steps to land between, so the slider
                             is the whole range and the number beside it says where in it the pen is.
                             The pen next to them is the same drawing the row shows, so the width that
                             is set can be read off it as it is dragged. -->
                        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto,Auto" ColumnSpacing="10">
                            <Slider x:Name="ThicknessSlider"
                                    Grid.Column="0"
                                    VerticalOptions="Center"
                                    ValueChanged="OnThicknessSliderChanged" />
                            <Label x:Name="ThicknessValue"
                                   Grid.Column="1"
                                   VerticalOptions="Center"
                                   Style="{StaticResource BodyLabel}" />
                            <controls:PenGlyphView x:Name="ThicknessPreview"
                                                   Grid.Column="2"
                                                   WidthRequest="26"
                                                   HeightRequest="48"
                                                   Style="{StaticResource PenGlyph}" />
                        </Grid>
                    </VerticalStackLayout>
                </ScrollView>
            </Border>
        </Grid>

        <!-- The radial menu covers the whole page so a press beside it can be told from one on it:
             it is transparent and lets touches through until it is opened, and the ring is drawn
             around the point it was opened at. Last child, so it is above the note it was asked for
             from. -->
        <ink:RadialMenuView x:Name="RadialMenu"
                            Grid.RowSpan="3"
                            BackgroundColor="Transparent"
                            InputTransparent="True" />
    </Grid>
</ContentPage>
